<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasili IV of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1552">1552</a>–<a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Vasily Shuisky, Vasili Shuisky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasili IV Shuisky was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1606 to 1610, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. A leading boyar of a Rurikid princely family, he took the throne after the murder of the first False Dmitry but faced the Bolotnikov rebellion and a second pretender. His alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> provoked Polish intervention, and after the Russian defeat at Klushino in 1610 he was deposed, forced to become a monk and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where he died in captivity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasili-iv-of-russia.jpg" alt="Vasili IV of Russia" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collective of Kremlin armory · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasily_shuysky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
